@page "/accessibility"
@inject BmotionCapabilities Caps

<PageTitle>Accessibility and diagnostics - Bit.Bmotion</PageTitle>

<h1>Accessibility and diagnostics</h1>

<p class="page-intro">
    Motion is not free for everyone who sees it. For some people a page that slides, parallaxes and
    springs is nausea, not delight - which is why browsers ship
    <strong>prefers-reduced-motion</strong>. Bmotion honours it, but not by throwing every animation
    away: opacity and colour keep animating while transforms, layout and dimension changes snap.
    That is the difference between a page that stops moving and a page that stops working.
</p>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Reduced motion, four ways</h2>
    <p>
        Pick the policy once, at registration. <code>User</code> is the recommended setting and the
        web-platform default: follow the operating system everywhere. The default is
        <code>IgnoreUnlessConfigured</code>, which only consults the OS inside a
        <code>&lt;BmotionConfig&gt;</code> - a back-compatible choice, so an app that never opted in
        can never have its animations silently switched off underneath it.
    </p>

    <div class="a11y-table-wrap">
        <table class="a11y-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>BmReducedMotionMode</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var mode in _modes)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>@mode.Name</code></td>
                        <td>@mode.Description</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_modesCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>What "reduced" actually does</h2>
    <p>
        Both boxes below animate <code>x</code>, <code>rotate</code> <em>and</em>
        <code>backgroundColor</code>. The right one sits inside
        <code>&lt;BmotionConfig ReduceMotion="true"&gt;</code>: its position and rotation jump
        straight to the target while its colour still crossfades. Nothing is lost - the state change
        is still legible, it just does not travel across the screen.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">Normal</span>
            <Bmotion Animate='@(_shifted
                        ? Bm.To(x: 60, rotate: 180, backgroundColor: "#FD7F36")
                        : Bm.To(x: -60, rotate: 0, backgroundColor: "#1276C6"))'
                     Transition="Bm.Tween(0.8, BmEase.InOut)">
                <div class="box" />
            </Bmotion>
        </div>

        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">ReduceMotion="true"</span>
            <BmotionConfig ReduceMotion="true">
                <Bmotion Animate='@(_shifted
                            ? Bm.To(x: 60, rotate: 180, backgroundColor: "#FD7F36")
                            : Bm.To(x: -60, rotate: 0, backgroundColor: "#1276C6"))'
                         Transition="Bm.Tween(0.8, BmEase.InOut)">
                    <div class="box" />
                </Bmotion>
            </BmotionConfig>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="demo-actions">
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="@(() => _shifted = !_shifted)">Move both</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_reducedCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Slowing everything down</h2>
    <p>
        <code>TransitionSpeed</code> is a playback multiplier over a whole subtree, and it scales
        every time-based field - duration, delay, repeat delay, a spring's visual duration - so the
        animation stays in proportion rather than having its delays left behind. It is how you
        review timing at quarter speed without editing a single transition, and
        <code>TransitionSpeed="0"</code> is how you check what a page looks like with no motion at
        all.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        @foreach (var speed in _speeds)
        {
            <div class="demo-card stack">
                <span class="demo-label">TransitionSpeed: @speed</span>
                <BmotionConfig TransitionSpeed="speed">
                    <Bmotion @key="@($"{_runId}-{speed}")"
                             Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 40, scale: 0.5)"
                             Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0, scale: 1)"
                             Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.4, duration: 0.8)">
                        <div class="box" />
                    </Bmotion>
                </BmotionConfig>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="demo-actions">
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="@(() => _runId++)">Replay all</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_speedCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Keyboard-accessible taps</h2>
    <p>
        <code>WhileTap</code> is not a mouse-only state. When a tappable element has focus,
        <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Space</kbd> press and release it exactly like a pointer -
        <code>WhileTap</code> plays and <code>OnTap</code> fires, and losing focus mid-press cancels
        it the way dragging off the element does. Give the element <code>tabindex="0"</code> if it
        is not natively focusable. <strong>Tab</strong> to the box below and press
        <kbd>Space</kbd>.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">Tab to it, then press Enter or Space</span>
            <Bmotion WhileHover="Bm.To(scale: 1.08)"
                     WhileFocus="Bm.To(scale: 1.08)"
                     WhileTap="Bm.To(scale: 0.88)"
                     OnTap="@(() => _taps++)"
                     Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 400, damping: 20)">
                <div class="box" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Tap counter">@_taps</div>
            </Bmotion>
        </div>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_keyboardCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Knowing what the runtime can do</h2>
    <p>
        Rather than guessing which render mode you are in, inject <strong>BmotionCapabilities</strong>.
        On Blazor Server there is no synchronous interop, so the per-frame loop cannot run: drag,
        inertia, colour interpolation, keyframe arrays and motion values collapse to instant state
        changes, while compositor-eligible animations still play normally. Detect it and offer a
        different affordance instead of shipping a control that silently does not move.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">This runtime</span>
            <div class="caps-list">
                <span class="badge">SupportsFrameLoop: @Caps.SupportsFrameLoop</span>
                <span class="badge">SupportsCompositor: @Caps.SupportsCompositor</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_capsCode" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Watching the engine</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>OnUpdate</strong> hands you the exact CSS the engine flushed to an element this
        frame. It is a plain delegate rather than an <code>EventCallback</code> on purpose: it runs
        inside the render loop, so it must not trigger a re-render per frame - read it, and update
        the DOM yourself or throttle to state.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">Live transform, straight from the frame callback</span>
            <Bmotion Animate="Bm.To(x: [-70, 70, -70], rotate: [0, 180, 360])"
                     Transition="Bm.Tween(4, BmEase.InOut, repeat: BmRepeat.Forever)"
                     OnUpdate="OnFrame">
                <div class="box box-sm" />
            </Bmotion>
            <code class="frame-readout">@_lastFrame</code>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p>
        For everything at once there is <strong>&lt;BmotionInspector /&gt;</strong>: drop it
        anywhere in a development build and it lists every element registered with the engine, its
        active drivers and its live values. It is the answer to "is this element animating, and
        what does the engine think its transform is?" - a question that is otherwise very hard to
        ask from the outside.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-actions">
        <button class="btn-bm" @onclick="@(() => _inspector = !_inspector)">
            @(_inspector ? "Hide" : "Show") the inspector
        </button>
    </div>

    @if (_inspector)
    {
        <BmotionInspector RefreshMs="120" />
    }

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_diagnosticsCode" />
</div>

<style>
    .a11y-table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }

    .a11y-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .9rem; }

    .a11y-table th, .a11y-table td {
        padding: .6rem .75rem;
        text-align: left;
        vertical-align: top;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--bit-clr-brd-ter);
    }

    .a11y-table th { font-weight: 700; color: var(--bit-clr-fg-pri); }

    .a11y-table td { color: var(--bit-clr-fg-sec); }

    .caps-list { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5rem; justify-content: center; }

    /* Monospace and fixed-height so the per-frame readout does not reflow the card as the
       numbers change width. */
    .frame-readout {
        display: block;
        min-height: 2.5rem;
        max-width: 100%;
        overflow-wrap: anywhere;
        font-size: .72rem;
        text-align: center;
        color: var(--bit-clr-fg-sec);
    }
</style>

@code {
    private bool _shifted;
    private int _runId;
    private int _taps;
    private bool _inspector;
    private string _lastFrame = "";

    private sealed record Mode(string Name, string Description);

    private readonly Mode[] _modes =
    [
        new("IgnoreUnlessConfigured", "The default, for back-compatibility. The OS preference is respected only inside a <BmotionConfig>."),
        new("User", "Recommended. Respect the OS preference everywhere - the web-platform default."),
        new("Always", "Always reduce, whatever the OS says. Useful for a per-app 'reduce motion' setting."),
        new("Never", "Never reduce. For surfaces where the motion carries the meaning."),
    ];

    private readonly double[] _speeds = [1, 0.35, 0.1];

    // Plain delegate, not an EventCallback: this runs inside the engine's frame loop, so it must
    // not re-render per frame. Here it only reads the transform into a field that the *next*
    // ordinary render happens to pick up.
    private void OnFrame(IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> css)
        => _lastFrame = css.TryGetValue("transform", out var t) ? t : string.Join("; ", css.Values);

    private const string _modesCode = """
        // Program.cs - pick the policy once.
        builder.Services.AddBitBmotionServices(o => o.ReducedMotion = BmReducedMotionMode.User);

        // IgnoreUnlessConfigured  the default: the OS preference matters only inside a config
        // User                    recommended: follow the OS everywhere
        // Always / Never          force it, whatever the OS says
        """;

    private const string _reducedCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* Both animate x, rotate AND backgroundColor. Under reduced motion the transform snaps
           while the colour still crossfades - Motion's "user" semantics, and a much better
           reduction than collapsing every property to instant. *@
        <BmotionConfig ReduceMotion="true">
            <Bmotion Animate='@(_shifted
                        ? Bm.To(x: 60, rotate: 180, backgroundColor: "#FD7F36")
                        : Bm.To(x: -60, rotate: 0, backgroundColor: "#1276C6"))'
                     Transition="Bm.Tween(0.8, BmEase.InOut)">
                <div class="box" />
            </Bmotion>
        </BmotionConfig>

        @* ReduceMotion="null" follows the OS instead of forcing either answer. *@

        @code {
            private bool _shifted;
        }
        """;

    private const string _speedCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* A playback multiplier over the whole subtree. Every time-based field scales together -
           duration, delay, repeat delay, a spring's visual duration - so the animation stays in
           proportion. 0 makes the subtree instant. *@
        <BmotionConfig TransitionSpeed="0.35">
            <Bmotion Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 40, scale: 0.5)"
                     Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0, scale: 1)"
                     Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.4, duration: 0.8)">
                <div class="box" />
            </Bmotion>
        </BmotionConfig>
        """;

    private const string _keyboardCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* Enter and Space press and release a focused tappable element exactly like a pointer:
           WhileTap plays, OnTapStart/OnTap fire, and losing focus mid-press cancels. The only
           thing you supply is focusability. *@
        <Bmotion WhileHover="Bm.To(scale: 1.08)"
                 WhileFocus="Bm.To(scale: 1.08)"
                 WhileTap="Bm.To(scale: 0.88)"
                 OnTap="@(() => _taps++)"
                 Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 400, damping: 20)">
            <div class="box" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Tap counter">@_taps</div>
        </Bmotion>

        @code {
            private int _taps;
        }
        """;

    private const string _capsCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion
        @inject BmotionCapabilities Caps

        @* SupportsFrameLoop is false on Blazor Server, where drag, inertia, colour interpolation,
           keyframe arrays and motion values degrade to instant state changes. Branch on it rather
           than shipping a control that silently does not move. *@
        @if (Caps.SupportsFrameLoop)
        {
            <Bmotion Drag="true"><div class="box" /></Bmotion>
        }
        else
        {
            <p>Dragging needs Blazor WebAssembly - use the buttons instead.</p>
        }
        """;

    private const string _diagnosticsCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* OnUpdate receives the CSS actually flushed to this element this frame. A plain delegate,
           not an EventCallback: it runs inside the frame loop and must not re-render per frame. *@
        <Bmotion Animate="Bm.To(x: [-70, 70, -70], rotate: [0, 180, 360])"
                 Transition="Bm.Tween(4, BmEase.InOut, repeat: BmRepeat.Forever)"
                 OnUpdate="OnFrame">
            <div class="box" />
        </Bmotion>

        @* The whole engine at once: every registered element, its active drivers and live values.
           Drop it into a development build. *@
        <BmotionInspector RefreshMs="120" />

        @code {
            private string _lastFrame = "";

            private void OnFrame(IReadOnlyDictionary<string, string> css)
                => _lastFrame = css.TryGetValue("transform", out var t) ? t : "";
        }
        """;
}
